E-E-A-T stands for Experience, Expertise, Authoritativeness, and Trustworthiness, the framework Google's quality raters use to judge whether a page deserves to rank. It is not a ranking factor in the way a backlink or a title tag is a ranking factor. Google has said repeatedly that E-E-A-T does not feed into the algorithm directly: it trains the raters, and the raters' judgements train the ranking systems. Most content published about E-E-A-T misses that distinction, and a fair amount of it fits the pattern Google's own John Mueller called digital mulch in December 2025: a definition, four bullet points, generic advice to add an author bio. This post covers what each component actually means, how Google assesses it without a literal score, what changes for YMYL topics, where practitioners genuinely disagree about it, and why the same signals now decide whether AI Overviews and ChatGPT cite you at all.

## What Does Each Letter in E-E-A-T Stand For?

**What does E-E-A-T stand for?** Experience, Expertise, Authoritativeness, and Trustworthiness. Google's Search Quality Rater Guidelines use these four aspects to describe what "helpful, reliable, people-first content" looks like. The guidelines are explicit that trust is the most important of the four: the others feed into trust, but a page does not need to demonstrate all of them to be judged trustworthy. Google added the first E, Experience, in December 2022, splitting it out from Expertise to recognise that first-hand contact with a topic is a different kind of credibility to academic or professional knowledge.

## Is E-E-A-T a Google Ranking Factor?

No, not directly, and this is the part most explainers get wrong. Quality raters do not rank live search results. They score sample pages against the guidelines, and Google uses those scores to check whether its ranking systems are surfacing the kind of content the guidelines describe. A rater's score on your page does not change your position for that page. It changes how Google tunes the algorithm over time. There is no "E-E-A-T score" sitting in Search Console or any third-party tool, whatever a rank tracker's dashboard implies.

What does move rankings are the signals that happen to correlate with high E-E-A-T: citations from sites Google already trusts, a satisfying answer that stops someone pogo-sticking back to the results, accurate structured data, a domain history free of manual actions. Improve those and you improve E-E-A-T and rankings together, which is exactly why the two get conflated. Google's own staff have pushed back on this for years: you cannot add E-E-A-T to a page the way you add a meta description. Bylines on content nobody actually wrote, "reviewed by" badges with no real reviewer behind them, and generic About pages copied from a template do not create the underlying reality the guidelines are trying to detect.

## What is Experience in E-E-A-T?

**What is Experience in Google E-E-A-T?** Experience is first-hand, direct contact with the thing being discussed. A product review written by someone who has used the product demonstrates experience. A destination guide written by someone who has visited the place does too. Content aggregated from other sources without any direct contact with the topic does not, however well it is written.

Experience signals include specific personal detail a template could not produce: photographs the author took themselves, a result that did not go to plan and why, timing and context only someone who was there would know. For a service business, this looks like case studies with real project detail and measured outcomes rather than a generic description of what the service involves. Across my own portfolio, this is the one component I can point to and say it changed something concrete: pages that read as though I had actually run the site being described, rather than researched it, are the pages that hold their position when a core update lands.

## What is Expertise in E-E-A-T?

**What is Expertise in Google E-E-A-T?** Expertise is the depth of knowledge and skill the content demonstrates. It can come from formal qualifications, from professional experience, or from what the guidelines call everyday expertise: knowledge built from doing something repeatedly, without a certificate to show for it. Google applies a noticeably higher bar to expertise on YMYL topics (Your Money or Your Life: content about finance, health, safety, or legal matters, where being wrong can cause real harm) than it does to a hobby blog.

Demonstrating expertise in practice means author bios with credentials a reader could actually verify, content that goes past the surface-level answer, technically accurate detail, and a structure that shows you understand the methodology, not only the topic. [Entity SEO](/blog/what-is-entity-seo/), the practice of making search engines and AI systems recognise who or what you are as a distinct, connected entity, is what makes these expertise signals machine-readable rather than something a rater has to infer from prose alone.

## What is Authoritativeness in E-E-A-T?

**What is Authoritativeness in SEO?** Authoritativeness is the reputation your entity, whether that's you, your brand, or your site, has within a specific topic area, as recognised by sources outside your own website. It is built by mentions, citations, links, and references from other entities Google already considers authoritative in that space, not by anything you say about yourself.

This is where [topical authority](/services/topical-authority/) and E-E-A-T overlap. Publishing expert content consistently, earning citations from sources with existing authority, and covering a topic comprehensively rather than in isolated posts all demonstrate authoritativeness to raters and algorithms at the same time.

## What is Trustworthiness in E-E-A-T?

**What is Trustworthiness in Google E-E-A-T?** Trustworthiness is the aspect the guidelines call most important: whether users and Google can rely on your content, your site, and your entity to be accurate, honest, and safe. It covers security (HTTPS), transparency (clear authorship, contact details, a real privacy policy), factual accuracy, and the absence of deceptive design.

Trust signals include HTTPS, accessible contact information, an About page that says something specific rather than something generic, author attribution with verifiable credentials, source citations for factual claims, and no misleading content or dark patterns. Because trust sits underneath the other three, fixing it tends to have the broadest effect: a site that is honest about what it is and who runs it clears the bar for the other components almost automatically.

## E-E-A-T and YMYL: Why the Bar Moves

YMYL topics get more scrutiny because getting them wrong has real consequences. Everything else gets more latitude.

| | YMYL topics (health, finance, legal, safety) | Everything else |
|---|---|---|
| Expertise expected | Formal credentials: medical, legal, or financial qualifications | Everyday expertise: direct, lived experience is enough |
| Rater scrutiny | High. Low-quality content here gets the guidelines' lowest ratings | Lower. More room for personal and hobbyist content |
| What raises the bar further | Content that could cause real financial or physical harm if it's wrong | Content where being wrong mostly wastes someone's time |

The practical upshot: a review of a coffee grinder and a review of a mortgage product are not held to the same standard, and treating them as though they are wastes effort. Spend the credentialing and citation work where the guidelines actually demand it.

## The Signals That Actually Move Rankings

Work on E-E-A-T at the entity level, not page by page. These are the actions I've found actually change something, rather than ticking a guideline box.

**A specific About page.** State who you are, your qualifications, your location, and your actual area of expertise. This is the primary entity definition document on a site and the first place a rater, and increasingly an AI system, looks to answer "who wrote this."

**Named authors with real bylines, not placeholder ones.** Attribute content to a real person with a bio describing genuine expertise, linked to a LinkedIn profile or published work a reader can check. Google's own guidance for helpful content is explicit that it wants bylines to be self-evident where a reader would expect one, and that authorship information should be accurate, not decorative.

**First-hand experience signals.** Case studies, real outcomes, and specifics only someone with direct contact with the topic could include.

**External authority you did not write yourself.** Mentions, citations, and links from established sources in your industry. A [structured approach to building topical authority](/blog/how-to-build-topical-authority/) accelerates this because comprehensive coverage earns citations faster than isolated posts do.

**Structured data that matches reality.** Organisation, Person, and Article schema communicate entity attributes in a format machines can read directly, reinforcing whatever is already true in the content rather than substituting for it.

## Where SEOs Get E-E-A-T Wrong

A few points of genuine disagreement and confusion come up often enough to be worth naming directly, rather than pretending the topic is settled.

**Treating E-E-A-T as a checklist you can complete.** Google has pushed back on this specific framing for years: E-E-A-T describes a quality the guidelines are trying to detect, not a set of fields to fill in. Adding an author bio to content nobody actually wrote does not create expertise, it adds a paragraph that fails to match reality if anyone checks.

**The famous author vs unknown author tension.** Google has said there is no measurable ranking difference between a well-known author's byline and an unknown one, all else equal. At the same time, early data on AI citation patterns leans toward named, verifiable authors over anonymous ones. Both things can be true at once: rankings and AI citation are not the same system, and conflating them is a common mistake.

**Fake credentialing shows up more than people admit.** "Medically reviewed by" badges with no real reviewer, or a "20 years of experience" author box on a site that has existed for three, are common enough in competitive niches that I would treat any credential I cannot verify with a two-minute search as a red flag on a competitor's page and a liability on my own.

**Not every ranking drop is an E-E-A-T problem.** It gets blamed for declines that are actually query drift, a core update reweighting something unrelated, or a competitor publishing something better. Diagnose from the data before assuming the fix is a credentials page.

## E-E-A-T and AI Search: Why It Matters More Now

AI Overviews, Google's AI-generated answer summaries shown above the normal results, and chat-based search tools like ChatGPT and Perplexity all have to decide which sources to trust before they decide what to say. That selection step draws on the same underlying signals E-E-A-T describes: is this a real, identifiable source, does it demonstrate direct contact with the topic, would a human quality rater consider it reliable.

Getting cited is now as much about entity credibility as it is about [answer structure](/blog/optimise-content-for-ai-search/). A cross-platform audit of my own portfolio in February 2026 found over 120,000 Bing Copilot citations across 11 sites, a ratio of roughly 57 AI citations for every organic click, and the single highest-cited page was a technical guide with specific, checkable detail rather than a generic overview.

One honest caveat: search "E-E-A-T AI Overviews" right now and you'll find a wave of 2026-dated posts citing suspiciously precise numbers, a specific percentage of citations requiring certain signals, a specific correlation coefficient between authority and citation rate, attributed to no named study or primary source. I could not trace a single one of those figures back to Google, Bing Webmaster Tools, or a published methodology while researching this piece. Treat any AI-citation statistic you cannot trace to a primary source as noise, not evidence, and measure your own citation rate through Bing Webmaster Tools rather than someone else's unverifiable claim.

## Frequently Asked Questions

**Is E-E-A-T a Google ranking factor?**

Not directly. It is a framework in Google's Search Quality Rater Guidelines, used by human raters to score sample pages so Google can check whether its ranking systems are working as intended. Rater scores do not change where a specific page ranks. The signals that demonstrate E-E-A-T, such as citations, accurate structured data, and a satisfying answer, do correlate with rankings, which is why the two get treated as the same thing when they are not.

**How do you add E-E-A-T to a website?**

You cannot add it directly, because it describes a quality of the content and the entity behind it, not a field you fill in. What you can do is make the reality more visible: a specific About page, real named authors with verifiable bios, first-hand detail in the content itself, accurate schema markup, and external citations earned rather than fabricated. Adding a bio to content nobody actually wrote does not create expertise, it asserts something false.

**Does E-E-A-T apply to every page, or only YMYL content?**

It applies to everything, but the bar moves. YMYL topics, meaning content about finance, health, legal, or safety matters, are held to a formal-expertise standard because getting them wrong can cause real harm. Everything else gets more latitude for everyday expertise, meaning direct, lived experience without formal credentials.

**Does a named author actually help E-E-A-T?**

It helps establish the transparency and accuracy the guidelines call for, and Google's own guidance encourages accurate bylines where readers would expect one. What it does not do, according to Google, is give a well-known author's name a measurable ranking boost over an unknown one purely for being recognisable. The value is in the honesty of the attribution, not the fame of the name.

**Does E-E-A-T affect AI Overviews and ChatGPT citations?**

Yes, indirectly, in the same way it affects traditional rankings. AI systems have to select trustworthy sources before generating an answer, and the signals they lean on, identifiable authorship, direct experience with the topic, accurate entity data, overlap heavily with E-E-A-T. Treat unverified statistics about exact citation percentages with scepticism; track your own AI citation performance through Bing Webmaster Tools instead.

